+A \defn{free cut} is a finite family of pairwise disjoint free subterm
+occurrences, recording the typing context and type of each selected occurrence.
+The empty family is allowed. A cut can pass through a binder only when the bound
+variable is absent from every selected context. Strong sequentiality is equivalently
+expressed by requiring distinct external labels in every free cut of every subterm.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Controlled Subject Expansion]
+\llem{strong_subject_expansion}
+Fix a partition-based substitution $\tmthree = \subs{\tm}{\var}{\ls{\tmtwo}}$.
+If $\conof{\tmthree}$ is strongly sequential and $\lab$ is fresh for all labels
+of $\conof{\tmthree}$, including labels inside its types, then
+$\conof{(\lamp{\lab}{\var}{\tm})\ls{\tmtwo}}$ is strongly sequential.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+We track whole cuts, including their contexts, rather than only the root types
+of the reconstructed subterms. Induction on the partition substitution proves two facts.
+First, any free cut of the body gives a cut of its contractum with the same selected
+types; the contexts of variables bound above the substitution point are unchanged.
+The same holds for cuts inside each argument. Thus distinctness of external labels
+in the contractum implies distinctness in the body and arguments.
+Second, a cut of the body whose contexts omit $\var$ can be combined with arbitrary
+free cuts of the arguments to give a cut of the contractum with exactly the same
+selected contexts and types. The application case uses the resource partition:
+selections in different branches remain disjoint. The abstraction case uses the
+absence of its bound variable from the selected contexts.
+
+Consequently, every free cut of the expanded redex corresponds to a cut of
+$\tmthree$ with the same contexts and types, after removing at most the newly
+introduced abstraction. Selecting the whole redex corresponds to selecting
+$\tmthree$, by Subject Expansion and Linearity. If the new abstraction is selected,
+its external label is $\lab$; freshness separates it from all other selected labels.
+These facts also apply to proper subterms.
+
+To check correctness, observe that all inherited variable annotations have sequential
+types, and a new variable annotation is the type of a consumed argument.
+Free occurrences of any one variable form a cut, so their external labels are distinct;
+this gives sequential contexts and the sequential domain of the new abstraction.
+No old lambda is erased or copied by the partition substitution, and $\lab$ is fresh,
+so lambda labels remain unique. Typability is supplied by Subject Expansion.
+
+The cut correspondence extends through $\con$: unchanged branches retain their cuts,
+and a binder can be crossed because the selected old contexts are identical.
+Hence separation holds also between reconstructed subterms and their unchanged
+siblings. Freshness is taken against the entire $\conof{\tmthree}$, so the new label
+cannot collide with a sibling label. This proves both correctness and strong
+sequentiality in the surrounding context.
+Finally, the reconstructed binder domain is sequential, so its partition substitution
+agrees with the alternative type-directed substitution.
+\end{proof}
